For the tool_wrappers.rs change, I need a scope guard pattern. I'll create a small struct that clears the tool name on drop. Since the `execute` method is async, the scope guard needs to work with async. Actually, the simplest approach is to use a local struct that implements Drop, created before the inner.execute call. In async code, Drop is still called when the future is dropped, and panics during the await will still trigger Drop.

Wait, there's a subtlety. In async Rust, if a panic occurs inside the `inner.execute().await` call, the panic unwinds through the `execute` function, and the scope guard's Drop will be triggered. This is correct.

However, there's a concern about what happens if the panic occurs inside `execute` but before we set the tool name. The task says to set it before `self.inner.execute()` and clear after. Actually, looking more carefully at the async execute method, the guard will work correctly with async — the Drop will be called when the future is dropped, or when the function exits normally.

But wait — if we create the guard before calling `self.inner.execute(params, ctx).await`, the guard will be alive during the await. If the future is cancelled (dropped), the guard's Drop will run, clearing the tool name. If the future completes normally, the guard will also drop, clearing the tool name. If the future panics, unwinding will drop the guard. All paths are covered.

